Subject: [PATCH] test(backend): give each g3 door its own question, and force
 the admission race without a barrier (ORD-0224 step 6 follow-up 2)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two instrument defects in the door I added at 8f1f4d4, both found by measuring my own claims:

1. Cross-door coupling. In the campaign's run shape (many nodeids in one pytest process) mutant Y1a
   reddened `test_g3e_…`, while the same door run alone under Y1a passed. The five g3 doors all asked
   about `UNSEEN_COUNTY`, so they shared one fingerprint - and a worker released by an earlier door is
   still alive while a later door measures its burst. Each door now asks its own unnamed-destination
   question (`_own_question`, still unresolved, still the same code path).
2. The barrier could starve. Parking callers inside `resolve_destination` assumed all five resolve;
   with the pre-resolution read live, a caller that arrives after a commit never does, so the barrier
   went under-populated and the door died on its own 10 s timeout (measured: combined run 12.3 s and
   red, other batches green). Replaced with `test_g3e_admission_race_no_caller_may_see_another_before_commit`:
   for the burst each caller's first two `find_open_operation` answers are "nothing open yet" - exactly
   what the earliest interleaving looks like to a caller deciding alone - while the loser's read-back
   inside the `IntegrityError` handler stays unblinded. What is left able to deliver "one operation,
   five callers" is the partial unique index plus that read-back, i.e. the mechanism under test.

Readings at this commit: gates + weak-fix doors + measurement package = 20 collected,
19 passed / 1 failed (`test_g5…`, pre-existing, ORD-0218) on three consecutive combined runs, and the
10 s stall is gone (2.1-2.4 s).
